# The lumpy layers whose quantum can actually divide something. One row per lumpy layer whose
# quantum is above zero at every point.
#
# A quantum of zero divides nothing
#   So "the nameplate is a whole number of quanta" has no population on such a layer: every number
#   is a multiple of zero or none is, depending on which convention you reach for, and neither
#   answer is about the supply. This relation is the honest base for that rule, and counting every
#   lumpy layer instead would swell it with rows the arithmetic cannot touch.
#
# Above zero at its low
#   The rule reads each point of the nameplate against the same point of the quantum, so a quantum
#   whose low is zero leaves its lowest point with nothing to divide by. The low is the test: a
#   quantum's three points are in order, so a low above zero puts the other two above it.
-- pm:LumpyQuantum/size, strictly positive at its low.
SELECT l.*
FROM (
    :compose(layers/lumpy.sqlc)
) l
WHERE l.quantum_low > 0
